Why Are Advanced Etch Systems Becoming More Important?
Gate-all-around transistor structures, stacked NAND memory and other three-dimensional device architectures create difficult removal steps. Engineers need to clear a target material from a narrow opening while minimizing damage to an adjacent layer. In some cases, the relevant metric is microscopic profile uniformity; elsewhere, the decisive requirement is chemical selectivity, throughput or low particle generation.

Fab operators also face a system-level trade-off. A chamber that improves selectivity but adds lengthy cycle times can disrupt line capacity. Another chamber may process wafers quickly but introduce cleaning downtime or expensive consumable use. Advanced-node adoption therefore creates opportunities for tool suppliers that can demonstrate both physical precision and dependable manufacturing economics.

